1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,598
tstack is nine agent skills that Theo Browne uses every day, now public.

2
00:00:06,148 --> 00:00:08,134
They work in Claude Code and Codex,

3
00:00:08,134 --> 00:00:12,349
and each one teaches the agent one part of the pull request workflow.

4
00:00:12,499 --> 00:00:16,590
You start one by saying its name, like full send, or babysit.

5
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:20,760
Most of them chain into one pipeline.

6
00:00:20,910 --> 00:00:25,120
Make the change. Review it with sol loop. File the PR.

7
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:29,318
Babysit it through CI and review bots. Then merge.

8
00:00:29,768 --> 00:00:33,866
Full send runs that whole pipeline. First, the pieces.

9
00:00:35,667 --> 00:00:40,243
Sol loop gets your diff reviewed by OpenAI's GPT Sol, in rounds.

10
00:00:40,393 --> 00:00:44,182
Sol tags each finding as a blocker, a should, or a nit.

11
00:00:44,332 --> 00:00:49,300
The agent fixes the real bugs with the smallest change and pushes back on the rest with evidence,

12
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:51,392
tracking every finding in a ledger.

13
00:00:51,542 --> 00:00:54,744
Three rounds by default, up to five for risky code.

14
00:00:54,744 --> 00:00:58,560
It stops when Sol approves, or hands the dispute to you.

15
00:01:00,367 --> 00:01:03,703
File PR opens a pull request that leads with the problem.

16
00:01:03,853 --> 00:01:07,585
The title says why the change matters, not how it was built.

17
00:01:07,735 --> 00:01:10,611
The description opens with the problem in plain words,

18
00:01:10,611 --> 00:01:13,148
not a list of every function that changed.

19
00:01:13,598 --> 00:01:19,252
It checks for secrets, then opens a real PR, not a draft, so review bots run.

20
00:01:21,067 --> 00:01:25,983
The babysit PR skill watches a pull request through CI and review bots.

21
00:01:26,133 --> 00:01:31,812
It uses one watcher per PR: T3 Code's watch tool, or the bundled pr-wait script.

22
00:01:31,812 --> 00:01:34,242
Never a homemade polling loop.

23
00:01:34,392 --> 00:01:38,080
It checks each bot finding against the source, fixes the real ones,

24
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:41,132
and answers false positives with a written reason.

25
00:01:41,282 --> 00:01:45,450
It rebases when the main branch moves, and only merges if you ask.

26
00:01:47,267 --> 00:01:52,779
The leaving PR comments skill loads on its own whenever the agent posts on GitHub for you.

27
00:01:52,929 --> 00:01:56,947
Each comment opens with a note naming the model and who it speaks for.

28
00:01:57,097 --> 00:01:59,056
The reply leads with the conclusion,

29
00:01:59,056 --> 00:02:02,077
cites the code or the check, and skips the filler.

30
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:06,463
When you are walking away, say full send.

31
00:02:06,613 --> 00:02:12,197
The agent finishes the change, proves it, runs sol loop, files the PR, babysits it,

32
00:02:12,197 --> 00:02:13,439
and merges it.

33
00:02:13,589 --> 00:02:15,418
Proof is evidence it saw itself:

34
00:02:15,418 --> 00:02:19,717
a reproduction that now passes, the repo's checks, or the running app.

35
00:02:19,867 --> 00:02:24,432
It merges only when the gate passes: proof covers the diff, checks are green,

36
00:02:24,432 --> 00:02:28,586
findings are handled, and the branch is current. No admin bypass.

37
00:02:28,736 --> 00:02:31,452
If it gets stuck, it asks through a question tool,

38
00:02:31,452 --> 00:02:33,980
so the thread shows it is waiting on you.

39
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:39,159
Full stop does the same work, but stops at the gate.

40
00:02:39,309 --> 00:02:41,412
It turns off auto-merge, never merges,

41
00:02:41,412 --> 00:02:45,365
and hands you a PR that is ready, waiting on approval, or blocked.

42
00:02:47,167 --> 00:02:52,546
Council brings in three reviewers from three providers, such as Claude Opus, GPT Sol,

43
00:02:52,546 --> 00:02:53,795
and Gemini Pro.

44
00:02:53,945 --> 00:02:56,359
Each one scores the work out of ten.

45
00:02:56,509 --> 00:02:58,393
It applies the fixes it agrees with,

46
00:02:58,393 --> 00:03:02,827
and runs fresh rounds until all three are satisfied, without growing the scope.

47
00:03:02,977 --> 00:03:05,876
The scores go in a table in the PR description.

48
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:12,422
Modernize brings an old PR up to date, and asks whether it should still exist.

49
00:03:12,572 --> 00:03:15,954
It reads what changed on main since the branch split off.

50
00:03:16,104 --> 00:03:19,788
Then it picks a verdict: modernize, rework, or close.

51
00:03:19,938 --> 00:03:21,670
Only modernize gets a rebase,

52
00:03:21,670 --> 00:03:25,314
checked with git range-diff and pushed with force-with-lease.

53
00:03:25,314 --> 00:03:27,824
It never merges or closes on its own.

54
00:03:29,633 --> 00:03:33,751
Takeover finishes someone else's PR, branch, or agent thread.

55
00:03:33,901 --> 00:03:39,140
It treats the handoff as context, not proof, and checks the original failure itself.

56
00:03:39,290 --> 00:03:44,177
It carries the original commits onto a new branch and keeps the original authors' credit.

57
00:03:44,327 --> 00:03:47,890
Then it modernizes, files, and babysits the result.

58
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:51,202
Here is how they connect.

59
00:03:51,352 --> 00:03:57,109
Full stop builds on full send, which uses sol loop, file PR, and babysit PR.

60
00:03:57,259 --> 00:04:01,379
Takeover uses modernize, file PR, and babysit PR.

61
00:04:01,529 --> 00:04:06,034
Every skill that comments on GitHub goes through leaving PR comments.

62
00:04:06,034 --> 00:04:07,706
Council stands alone.

63
00:04:09,833 --> 00:04:10,512
To install,

64
00:04:10,512 --> 00:04:16,177
clone the tstack repo and copy its skills into your Claude Code or Codex skills folder.

65
00:04:16,327 --> 00:04:19,854
You need an authenticated GitHub CLI and Python 3.

66
00:04:20,004 --> 00:04:21,461
Then make them yours:

67
00:04:21,461 --> 00:04:27,172
put personal settings in CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, so updates do not erase them.

